 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>The baseline quotient of fantasy is usually pretty high in Alice + Olivia collections. This time, enabled by AI that has unlocked more imaginative capability for her, designer Stacey Bendet unleashed fantasy fully with a collection themed around “Alice in Wonderland.”</p><p>“I will literally sit and play with all the embroidery using AI myself,” said Bendet. “Things that used to take me back and forth with my design team for weeks, I can just go ‘I want this.’”</p><p>Bendet’s tinkering resulted in a plethora of intricate prints. A maximalist, multicolored, ultra-embellished garden scene covered a trio of looks: a classic bulbous ball gown, an opera coat, and a scarf top paired with a midiskirt. Gilded floral motifs detailed two regal dresses, one, with a train that cascaded down the steps, was worn by Bendet’s 15-year-old daughter. (The magical moment between girlhood and womanhood has also been on Bendet’s mind, she said.)</p><p>Bendet declared it was “the season of the dress,” but she also showed a few lacy separates, button-up and blazer combos, and bedazzled and be-ribboned, limited-edition white tank tops—“back to the early 2000s,” she said. A giant high-heeled sculpture in her Park Avenue presentation space helped announce her relaunch of footwear. The brand will soon offer a range of colorful pumps, slingbacks, platforms, and ballet flats “at a contemporary price point,” she said.</p><p>As her bustling presentation evidenced, there are plenty of people with whom Bendet’s idea of fantasy resonates.</p></div>",
